            <ArticleTitle>Omental infarction preceded by anatomically upturned omentum.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1125-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.clinimag.2013.07.005</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0899-7071(13)00163-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report the case of a 49-year-old man who presented with acute abdominal pain. Contrast-enhanced computed tomography of the abdomen revealed spontaneous omental torsion with no other noticeable findings. Notably, a computed tomography exam 6 months prior demonstrated the omentum located within the anterior hepatic space, suggesting that the patient had a hypermobile, upturned omentum. To our knowledge, this is the first case report illustrating an anatomically upturned omentum as precursor to omental infarction.</AbstractText>
